WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:03.570
Film Courage: How did you get your first Hollywood
writing job? Glenn Gers, Screenwriter: It

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:10.270
was a fluke, a freak occurrence. It was actually
a script that I had written 10 years before.

00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:14.920
It was my first script and I had gotten an
agent with my first script. I'd gotten very

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:19.190
lucky. I knew someone who knew someone who
showed it to an agent who picked me up as

00:00:19.190 --> 00:00:23.750
their client but they could not get me. There
had been a very small option during the 10

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:29.120
years other than that I was office temping
and administrative assistanting and doing

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:36.360
some development work for indie companies
and I was beginning to think I should stop

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:40.390
because I was actually planning to become
a teacher, like maybe a high school teacher

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:45.081
or college teacher if I could because at least
I would have summer vacations to write. It

00:00:45.081 --> 00:00:50.649
was always what can I do to support my writing
that never stopped but the idea of becoming

00:00:50.649 --> 00:00:55.180
a screenwriter stopped because I was like
why keep beating my head against the wall?

00:00:55.180 --> 00:01:00.100
I had written a bunch of scripts, I had an
agent. If I wasn't making it maybe I should

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:06.670
quit um but the the first script i had written
all of my scripts were well liked by the people

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:10.920
who read them they just were like this is
really good we just can't make it for a billion

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:16.270
reasons there's always a billion reasons why
people can't do things um but a an independent

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:21.340
producer who really loved one of my scripts
and i had done some rewrites with her um her

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:28.939
name was mae wathridge and she um took a job
at tribeca films when it was just starting

00:01:28.939 --> 00:01:36.229
and um she had to say that she was stopping
her independent career and going to work for

00:01:36.229 --> 00:01:43.439
tribeca but she had the opportunity uh she
met with someone who works with billy crystal

00:01:43.439 --> 00:01:48.170
who was looking to work with de niro on uh
what turned out to be analyzed this eventually

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:53.530
after i failed them um but they they were
looking to work together and may slipped them

00:01:53.530 --> 00:02:01.810
my script and they loved it and they said
this this would work very well and so all

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:08.289
of a sudden tribeca had to pick me up as like
oh i guess we'll pick up his script to take

00:02:08.289 --> 00:02:15.000
so all of a sudden from being about to quit
i was working with big hollywood stars and

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:20.849
my agent lisa calamaro arranged a fabulous
option and first step deal and all of a sudden

00:02:20.849 --> 00:02:26.769
i didn't have to work temp for a year while
i did rewrites on that project which went

00:02:26.769 --> 00:02:33.989
so fast downhill and died completely and the
um they wanted changes that i didn't know

00:02:33.989 --> 00:02:39.400
how to make and i had worked for 10 years
learning how to write what was in my head

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:44.609
and i had never learned about the part of
hollywood writing where somebody says change

00:02:44.609 --> 00:02:51.099
that that wasn't even on my radar and this
was a long time ago this was in the late 1980s

00:02:51.099 --> 00:02:57.079
it just never occurred to me that like someone
would say as they did it was the story was

00:02:57.079 --> 00:03:02.459
about a divorcing couple a couple who had
gotten divorced and remarried and um divorced

00:03:02.459 --> 00:03:10.180
again and over political differences and then
the guy gets involved in espionage and politics

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:15.510
and things and so their divorce the topic
of their divorce becomes the subject of the

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:22.120
thriller and so it was sort of a thriller
romantic comedy and the note was can you take

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:29.479
out the divorce and and it was a completely
legitimate note in one way if you were a flexible

00:03:29.479 --> 00:03:34.290
hollywood writer who understood how to rethink
things there are ways to do that and i actually

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:38.779
did that later on another script where i took
out what i considered the center of the story

00:03:38.779 --> 00:03:45.469
at the time i had never had that question
raised and i tried so hard i i was just like

00:03:45.469 --> 00:03:50.669
i got to do this this is my big break and
i'm blowing it um but i couldn't i wrote an

00:03:50.669 --> 00:03:55.499
entire script i've actually it's the only
thing i've ever written that i have never

00:03:55.499 --> 00:04:03.730
looked at since it's 30 years now i wrote
it i literally cried through much of the writing

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:08.969
like i was a basket case because i was writing
something that i didn't know what the center

00:04:08.969 --> 00:04:14.229
was because for me the center of this story
the reason that everyone was doing something

00:04:14.229 --> 00:04:19.229
was because of this couple trying to get back
together when you take that out all you've

00:04:19.229 --> 00:04:24.039
got is a bunch of people chasing each other
in funny ways but still wasn't enough to make

00:04:24.039 --> 00:04:32.680
a scene i wrote a script i have no idea if
it's readable but i got immediately fired

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:39.080
uh the script went on on the shelf and they
hired new writers and for three years they

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:46.710
held on to it and niro and crystal wisely
moved on did analyze this it was a good choice

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:53.020
on their part um and at the end of that three
years i got my script back which was cool

00:04:53.020 --> 00:05:00.300
because i had been well paid to let them have
it for three years i had never seen any of

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:06.120
the rewrites that were done after i did it
so therefore i was i was allowed to take everything

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:11.300
i had done that was my property the script
i the draft i done for them was their property

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:18.122
because they paid me for it but the option
was over and i just started sending it out

00:05:18.122 --> 00:05:24.990
again and i so many people loved it and got
so close to production so many times but what

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:30.509
it taught me was the thing that i thought
writing was about which is you imagine stuff

00:05:30.509 --> 00:05:34.949
and you get it down as good as you can and
then they pay you and they make it that's

00:05:34.949 --> 00:05:42.090
not how the industry works the industry works
that you do all that stuff and then you start

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:46.140
to collaborate with other people and if you
can't do that part if you can't learn how

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:54.100
to use those same creative skills on demand
for things you may not think are great then

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:59.289
they'll find someone who can does that sound
depressing it's not meant to it's meant to

00:05:59.289 --> 00:06:07.129
it's meant to say learn the mechanics of the
business so that you can do the job that's

00:06:07.129 --> 00:06:12.630
that's the message i hope is being sent there
is that there is the magic of creativity and

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:19.110
then there's the magic of being skilled and
knowing how to do the job no matter what gets

00:06:19.110 --> 00:06:22.820
thrown at you well i think it's very important
that you bring up that story because so many

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:27.659
ideas of something sound great and then when
you learn what they actually are it's much

00:06:27.659 --> 00:06:32.419
much different so that's just fascinating
so it was a year that you had to rewrite that

00:06:32.419 --> 00:06:38.889
or that was a year process it was a year of
actually this is it's a funny story so i'll

00:06:38.889 --> 00:06:45.009
tell it uh it's embarrassing um i got once
i took out the center of the story i didn't

00:06:45.009 --> 00:06:49.180
know what the story would be and i what i
do is that when i don't know what i'm going

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:53.690
to write i just riff i think of everything
i can i brainstorm it could be this it could

00:06:53.690 --> 00:06:57.490
be that you know we're on a submarine it could
be the submarine's at the bottom of the sea

00:06:57.490 --> 00:07:00.819
it could be at the submarine got moved to
dry land where you know everything i can possibly

00:07:00.819 --> 00:07:05.999
think i try and get it down on paper there's
no such thing as a bad idea until you've figured

00:07:05.999 --> 00:07:10.990
out what you're trying to accomplish with
it anything's possible so i had this huge

00:07:10.990 --> 00:07:16.669
list of possibilities of this story and i
didn't know which one they wanted and i was

00:07:16.669 --> 00:07:23.439
writing for them so um i made it into a board
game i actually i was working as an office

00:07:23.439 --> 00:07:27.860
temp still so i had access to like a laminating
machine everything and i made a board and

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:32.490
it was like it was like the game of life where
you go along a path but this had like multiple

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:38.389
branching paths for each story and and i tried
to lay out all the different story outlines

00:07:38.389 --> 00:07:43.020
as a board game with like cards like if you
pick this you go in this tray it was the most

00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:50.240
elaborate creative stupid thing i've ever
done and i brought it to billy crystal i got

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:56.139
an appointment i flew to los angeles i gave
it to him and i said this is your story outline

00:07:56.139 --> 00:08:01.340
i don't know which one you want and he was
really gracious about it and he was he was

00:08:01.340 --> 00:08:05.729
great he was like you know well now i have
something to put on the wall of my office

00:08:05.729 --> 00:08:09.870
but now we actually have to write the script
after work and he gave me advice and i worked

00:08:09.870 --> 00:08:15.779
on it and i did a draft and it was all fine
but that was how little i understood the job

00:08:15.779 --> 00:08:23.680
the job by the way is making choices the point
is when i when you lay out the 15 000 possible

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:29.300
stories you have to make a choice everything
is a choice every choice has a price you have

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:35.519
to learn to get comfortable with making choices
over and over and over again everything in

00:08:35.519 --> 00:08:41.270
creative work is i'm going to choose this
and not that if i choose this that means that

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:47.230
i can't have a happy ending if i choose this
it means i can't talk about communism versus

00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:51.899
capitalism you know whatever it is that you
want to do it's a choice and when you make

00:08:51.899 --> 00:08:58.139
that choice certain things fall away but other
things become stronger that's the creative

00:08:58.139 --> 00:09:04.389
process and everyone gets very afraid of that
what if i make the wrong choice the coolest

00:09:04.389 --> 00:09:09.190
thing is whatever a writer is to get comfortable
with you know what it's a choice i'm gonna

00:09:09.190 --> 00:09:14.940
make it i make it judging i know that the
producers want this well no more choice that's

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:20.010
what i'm doing if you don't know for sure
you change you take the choice and you follow

00:09:20.010 --> 00:09:25.269
that path you're gambling maybe it'll work
maybe it won't but it's better to make the

00:09:25.269 --> 00:09:33.160
choice and be creative and try and find your
solutions than to try and do both that's the

00:09:33.160 --> 00:09:38.470
one thing you can't do got to make a choice
and you have to live with the price when they

00:09:38.470 --> 00:09:43.430
asked you to take out which what was the center
of the story which is the couple getting divorced

00:09:43.430 --> 00:09:47.670
and getting back together did they didn't
tell you what to replace it with they didn't

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:53.149
know when people give you notes they don't
know how to fix it they they just like you

00:09:53.149 --> 00:09:57.699
know that seems seems a little wrong or sometimes
they'll know how to fix it but it's not what

00:09:57.699 --> 00:10:01.839
you thought would be like you might have a
better one it doesn't matter what matters

00:10:01.839 --> 00:10:09.920
is that everyone is going to see things differently
and most notes are this isn't working not

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:16.970
do this if they would do this then it's like
i'll do that most notes are that's not making

00:10:16.970 --> 00:10:23.089
sense or that's too slow or that's too fast
but how you actually solve it that's your

00:10:23.089 --> 00:10:30.140
task and so for you that was so new it never
occurred to me it was you know people didn't

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:35.160
know that much about screenwriting in the
late 80s early 90s like it wasn't as much

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:41.209
of a thing people didn't do it as much as
they do now now everybody writes scripts um

00:10:41.209 --> 00:10:47.110
and frankly everyone should because now everyone
can make them um but at the time the experience

00:10:47.110 --> 00:10:51.470
of being a professional writer was not very
well known um there were only a handful of

00:10:51.470 --> 00:10:58.690
books and in fact the first time i came to
la um i was my agent had come out and she

00:10:58.690 --> 00:11:04.630
had set me up with meetings and everyone said
to me your work is good you could work here

00:11:04.630 --> 00:11:10.070
if you moved here um but you would be have
to get used to writing things that didn't

00:11:10.070 --> 00:11:15.230
get made because at the time that was how
development worked they would develop 50 scripts

00:11:15.230 --> 00:11:19.360
and make one which meant 49 writers were writing
a script that was going to be put on a drawer

00:11:19.360 --> 00:11:23.040
never seen again and they would be highly
paid and they would be well respected and

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:27.800
maybe one of them would get lucky at one of
your scripts would would hit but you never

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:34.509
knew some people had a full career never getting
anything made and that blew my mind i was

00:11:34.509 --> 00:11:39.509
like i'm not going to work a whole year on
a script and not have anyone see it which

00:11:39.509 --> 00:11:46.050
of course that's the business that's the job
but at the time i didn't know i had no clue

00:11:46.050 --> 00:11:52.100
and it took me a while to process that and
to recognize that as a day job goes pretty

00:11:52.100 --> 00:11:57.110
cool you learn a lot on it and you get paid
to do the thing you love but it may get put

00:11:57.110 --> 00:12:02.940
on the shelf you may never see it again when
you came back to them with other ideas which

00:12:02.940 --> 00:12:07.760
could be the center of the story did they
not like it or that was the board game all

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:16.880
right this is the the process by which this
happens is that the people who are in charge

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:22.620
like if you're a star or a director and you're
supervising writers you know that you're not

00:12:22.620 --> 00:12:28.310
a writer and so you just try to guide them
as some are better than others and it's it's

00:12:28.310 --> 00:12:35.730
the writer's job to translate the notes that's
part of being a professional screenwriter

00:12:35.730 --> 00:12:39.199
is recognizing that people are going to tell
you stuff and you have to figure out what

00:12:39.199 --> 00:12:43.889
i believe is you have to figure out how to
turn it into scenes everything about screenwriting

00:12:43.889 --> 00:12:49.800
is how to think in scenes if you can figure
out how to make a scene out of an idea or

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:55.910
a desire or whatever it is that somebody says
then you can be a professional writer and

00:12:55.910 --> 00:13:02.139
um so very often the people in charge will
say like honestly you know i know what end

00:13:02.139 --> 00:13:07.150
result i want but i don't know how to get
there that's your job um so that was a lot

00:13:07.150 --> 00:13:11.600
of the notes were like that and in fact i
said i said here's the thing when it really

00:13:11.600 --> 00:13:16.699
comes down to it there's a big there's a binary
choice there's a this or that you know is

00:13:16.699 --> 00:13:23.290
it this ending or that ending and the note
was well could it be both

00:13:23.290 --> 00:13:28.140
what are you going to do so eventually i just
chose i actually think this board game is

00:13:28.140 --> 00:13:32.300
kind of cool but it was great i wish i don't
know if i have a copy of it i think i gave

00:13:32.300 --> 00:13:36.319
him the only copy that's kind of a neat idea
i mean if someone had the time to make the

00:13:36.319 --> 00:13:40.420
board game that that would be yeah absolutely
no i mean essentially it's create your own

00:13:40.420 --> 00:13:46.110
story which is now a completely legitimate
creative project you know you could you can

00:13:46.110 --> 00:13:50.320
you know digitally you can make a story that
you could do that with i think it's called

00:13:50.320 --> 00:13:55.770
a video game you know and um so i just was
decades ahead of my there you go i should

00:13:55.770 --> 00:13:56.770
have been designing games.

00:13:56.770 --> 00:13:57.279
